package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val storyBrothersWar = StorySection(
    "brothers-war", "Brothers' War",
    "Two brothers split a Thran stone in a desert cave, and the war that followed burned a continent and buried a world in ice for nearly three thousand years.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "the-thran", "The Thran",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "argive-orphans", "Argive Orphans",
                    listOf(
                        "[Urza](who/urza) was born on the first day of a year in Argive, one of the three coastal kingdoms of Terisiare, and his brother Mishra was born on the last day of the same year. Historians would later make that year the zero of their calendar and count everything after it from the brothers, which says a good deal about how the story ends. They were born into a noble family of Penregon. Their mother died while they were still small, and their father married another woman of the court, who treated the two boys with indifference on her better days and contempt on the rest.",
                        "In the brothers' tenth year their father fell ill. Years before, he had done a favor for an old friend, an Argivian scholar named Tocasia who spent her life digging in the deserts of Terisiare for the leavings of a vanished people. He called in that favor now. The boys were packed off to her excavation camp as apprentices, and their father died not long after, leaving them orphans with a noble name and nowhere to use it.",
                        "Tocasia had taken noble children before. Families sent them to her digs to toughen them up and teach them something of the world's past, and most of them went home again when the lesson was over. Urza and Mishra never went home. The camp became the only family either of them had, and Tocasia, gruff and patient and wholly absorbed in her work, became the nearest thing to a parent either boy had left. The cards remember her as {Tocasia, Dig Site Mentor}. She would also be the first person their rivalry killed.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Your father was a dear friend to whom I owe much. He wanted me to look after you, to care for you should something happen to him.",
                        "Tocasia's Welcome",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tocasias-dig", "Tocasia's Dig",
                    listOf(
                        "The camp sat in the desert and ran on two kinds of people. There were young nobles like the brothers, sent to learn, and there were the Fallaji, desert tribesmen who did most of the digging and knew the sand better than any scholar. Tocasia's students learned to read the ground, to map a site, and to handle whatever came out of it with care. The camp's rule against touching anything that glowed, hummed or whirred, preserved on {Meticulous Excavation}, existed for good reason. What came out of the ground was the Thran.",
                        "The Thran had ruled most of Terisiare thousands of years earlier. Nobody at the camp knew much more than that. They had left ruins half buried in the dunes, crystals that still held stored power, and machines whose purpose nobody could guess, some of which still moved when they were woken. How the Thran had lived in their floating capital of [Halcyon](halcyon), and how they had fallen to [one of their own](thran-phyrexian-war), nobody at the camp could have said.",
                        "The brothers grew up in opposite directions. Urza was cold and exacting and preferred the artifacts to the people who found them. He rarely dug. He studied, taking apart whatever the others brought up until he understood it. Mishra loved the camp and its people. He learned the diggers' ways and made friends among the Fallaji, among them a boy named Hajar who would stand by him through everything to come, and in time he oversaw all of the digging at the site.",
                        "The Fallaji had a way of describing a person as a balance of stone and fire, sky and water. Mishra, they said, had too much fire. Urza had too much stone, cold and unyielding, the kind that does not bend and so must one day shatter. Tocasia saw both brothers clearly, and she worried about what the two of them would do to each other.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The brothers are brilliant, but their quarreling will be the death of me.",
                        "Tocasia, journal entry, on Stern Lesson",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-ornithopter", "The Ornithopter",
                    listOf(
                        "The brothers made discoveries together long before they made war. The most famous was the ornithopter, a Thran flying machine with beating wings that the two of them found and took into the air. Later scholars would wonder whether the little machines printed as {Ornithopter} were Urza's first attempt at mechanical life, built in his apprentice days. Whatever their first form, they gave Tocasia's camp something no other dig had, a way to see the desert from above.",
                        "Urza's gift was pattern. Poring over the camp's records, he noticed that the sites richest in Thran devices were not scattered at random. Plotted on a map, they formed a ring, as though the Thran had built outward from some central place that nobody had ever found. If there was a heart to the vanished empire, it lay in the middle of that ring, out in country the diggers had never worked.",
                        "So Tocasia, Urza and Mishra climbed into the ornithopter and flew to the center of the ring. They found mountains, where the Sardian range met the Kher Ridges at the edge of the Western Desert, and among them the remains of a large Thran city. There were crumbling buildings and the dark mouths of caves. The three of them landed among the ruins and walked toward the middle of it all.",
                        "They had come looking for answers about the Thran. What waited for them underground in the caves of [Koilos](koilos) would end Tocasia's life, set her two students at each other's throats for half a century, and crack open a door that the last of the Thran had died to close.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Antiquities"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Brothers' War"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Brothers' War, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tocasia"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Brothers' War"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "koilos", "Koilos",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-ruined-city", "The Ruined City",
                    listOf(
                        "At the center of the ring there was not much left. A few crumbling buildings stood above a network of caves, and the caves were full of Thran machinery, silent under the dust of ages. The three explorers did not know it, but they were standing where [Halcyon](halcyon), the shining capital of the Thran, had once floated above the mountains. The city was long gone. What remained was what had lain beneath it, the caves the Thran had called the Caves of the Damned, where they had kept their sick and their poor.",
                        "The oldest tellings say it was [Urza](who/urza) who named the place, from an old Argivian word meaning secret, though later card text suggests the Thran already called it Koilos. Either way the name stuck, and it would be spoken with dread for thousands of years. Phyrexians would come through these caves to meddle in the Brothers' War, again to open the great [invasion](invasion) of Dominaria, and once more when [Sheoldred's](praetors) agents staged the second invasion there. The card {Caves of Koilos} shows the place echoing again with Phyrexians preparing for war.",
                        "None of that was visible on the first day. Tocasia saw an archaeologist's dream, a Thran site that no digger had ever touched. Mishra would soon begin to dream of a darkness that seemed to call him somewhere, but for now he simply followed his teacher and his brother underground, past machines nobody in the camp could have named, toward the deepest chamber of the caves.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "hall-of-tagsin", "Hall of Tagsin",
                    listOf(
                        "In a chamber later called the Hall of Tagsin they found a great machine, and set into it a powerstone of a size nobody at the camp had ever seen. Powerstones were the Thran's crystal batteries, mana trapped in stone, and every dig turned up chips and shards of them. This one was different. It sat on its pedestal as if it had been placed there with care, and it had been. The stone was holding something shut. The card {Hall of Tagsin} remembers the room as a place where the Thran's machines were only sleeping.",
                        "Five thousand years earlier [Rebbec](thran-phyrexian-war), the last architect of Halcyon, had laid that stone on that pedestal with her own hands, sealing a portal between Dominaria and the plane [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) had taken for his own. Nobody had told the brothers. Nobody alive knew.",
                        "Accounts of the moment differ, from a single explosion to a scramble for the stone, but every telling agrees that it broke in two and left one half in each brother's hand. What none of the three understood was that they had just broken the seal.",
                        "The portal behind it woke for a moment. Out of the dark came the su-chi, clanking constructs that set on the explorers and drove them out of the ruins. The card {Su-Chi} calls them flawed copies of Thran relics, unstable but useful to Tocasia's students, which is a kind way of describing machines that tried to kill them. Then the portal closed again on its own and the caves went quiet. Whatever lay on the far side of that door had been given one glimpse of Dominaria in five thousand years. It would not forget.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Thran's machines were not dead after all, merely dormant . . . and waiting.",
                        "Hall of Tagsin",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "two-stones", "Mightstone and Weakstone",
                    listOf(
                        "Back at camp the halves showed what they could do. Urza's half made things stronger than they had any right to be, machines and living things alike. Mishra's did the opposite, sapping the strength out of whatever it touched. It was Urza who chose the names, and they did his brother no favors. His own half became the {Mightstone}. His brother's became the {Weakstone}.",
                        "Neither brother could leave the other's half alone. The pieces had come from one stone, and each brother wanted it whole again, in his own hands. Tocasia saw the change in them before anyone else did and tried to defuse it. She asked them both to give her their stones, so that she could study the halves as a neutral party without the two of them arguing over every test. Neither would. Each was sure she would hand his half to the other.",
                        "There was more in the stones than either brother knew. The powerstone at Koilos had once been set inside the body of [a dying Thran artificer](thran-phyrexian-war), and something of him had remained in the crystal ever since. The brothers carried that ghost around the camp in their pockets and their fists. Mishra's dreams grew worse. Night after night the darkness he had felt in the caves seemed to pull at him, as if it wanted him back.",
                        "Tocasia kept the peace for as long as she could. It was only ever a matter of time before one brother tried to take the other's half.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "brotherhoods-end", "Brotherhood's End",
                    listOf(
                        "It happened at night. Mishra, drunk, went to his brother to take the Mightstone from him. Urza would not give it up, and the argument turned into a fight that the whole camp could hear. Then the brothers stopped using their fists and turned the stones on each other, the Mightstone's surge of strength meeting the Weakstone's drain.",
                        "Tocasia came running and did what a teacher does. She stepped between them. Both brothers lost their concentration at once, the forces pouring out of the stones broke loose, and the explosion killed her where she stood. The camp had a name for that night afterward, and the card {Brotherhood's End} still carries it.",
                        "Mishra fled. Ashamed of what he had caused, he ran into the desert in the confusion that followed, carrying the Weakstone and little else. Without Tocasia the dig fell apart. Her students scattered, and Urza walked away from the ruin of the only home he had known and went to the kingdom of Yotia to find work as a clockmaker's apprentice.",
                        "Neither brother ever forgave the other, and neither was ever free of the dead woman between them. Each now held half a Thran stone and the certainty that the other had stolen the rest. Out of that certainty, over the next half century, came [the war](the-war) that would wreck a continent.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The brothers' rivalry escalated into all-out enmity, and Tocasia paid the price.",
                        "Brotherhood's End",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Antiquities"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Brothers' War"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Brothers' War, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Koilos"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Mightstone"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Weakstone"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "the-war", "The War",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "yotia", "Yotia",
                    listOf(
                        "[Urza](who/urza) arrived in Yotia with nothing but his hands and his mind, and found work as a clockmaker's apprentice. Not long after, the Warlord of Kroog, ruler of Yotia, announced a contest. In a courtyard of his capital stood an enormous jade statue. Any man who could move it across the courtyard would marry his daughter, Kayla bin-Kroog, and with her would come a dowry that included an ancient Thran book.",
                        "Urza had no interest in the princess. He wanted the book. He built a machine big enough to shift the statue, won where every other contestant had failed, and married Kayla. On their wedding night he left her bed to go and read the Thran tome. The Warlord was pleased to have gained an artificer of that talent, and Urza became Yotia's Chief Artificer, with a workshop, assistants and a kingdom's treasury to spend. The card {Urza, Prince of Kroog} notes that he was happy to let Yotia's coffers fund his research.",
                        "In that workshop he built the ornithopters and the {Yotian Soldier} automatons that came to define the kingdom's army. He also gained an apprentice. A young toymaker named Tawnos came to Kroog from the town of Jorilin and won Urza's attention by setting a mechanical snake of his own making in front of him. Tawnos built machines that borrowed their designs from living creatures, and many of Urza's later inventions owed something to him. Over the years he became Urza's most trusted student, and a friend to Kayla when her husband was not.",
                        "Urza mostly ignored his wife in those early years. They grew closer only later, after her father's death, when Kayla saw that Urza blamed himself for it. By then the reason for the Warlord's death had a name, and it was Mishra.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "fallaji", "Fallaji",
                    listOf(
                        "Mishra had run into the desert with the Weakstone and a pull in his dreams toward Koilos. He never got there. A militant Fallaji tribe called the Suwwardi caught him, took the Weakstone and made him a slave. For a time he did whatever menial work he was given. Then Hajar, the Fallaji boy he had befriended at Tocasia's camp, found him and persuaded the Suwwardi leader, the Qadir, to make the young Argivian a tutor to his son. Mishra was still a slave, but a useful one.",
                        "The Fallaji prized self-reliance, and the Qadir began to worry that his son was leaning on his teacher. He was weighing whether to have Mishra killed when the desert decided for him. One night Mishra woke screaming from a dream of a mechanical parody of nature. Moments later a giant machine shaped like a dragon burst from under the sand and tore through the camp, killing the Qadir and many others. In the chaos Mishra and Hajar took the Weakstone back from the dead man, meaning to weaken the beast. Instead Mishra found that, with the stone, he could command it.",
                        "The Qadir's son became the new Qadir and raised Mishra from slave to wizard. Together they turned the dragon engine, the Mak Fawa, on the other Fallaji tribes and united them under the Suwwardi, a people who had always distrusted artifice now carried toward empire by a machine. The card {Mishra, Tamer of Mak Fawa} remembers him in that role.",
                        "Near the city of Zegon the campaign met an artificer whose staves could hold the dragon engine back. Her name was Ashnod. Mishra was fascinated by her work, she became his apprentice and his lover, and Zegon bought its survival by giving her up, tearing down its gates and submitting to the Fallaji. Ashnod was brilliant, cruel and loyal to Mishra alone, and the {Staff of Zegon} only hinted at what she could build. With Hajar at one side and Ashnod at the other, Mishra came to the borders of Yotia, where his brother was waiting.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "kroog", "Kroog",
                    listOf(
                        "The brothers met again at a peace summit in Korlinda, in the neutral kingdom of Korlis, Urza as Chief Artificer of Yotia and Mishra as wizard to the Qadir. It was the first time they had seen each other since Tocasia's camp, and for a moment they were glad of it. Then the Warlord of Kroog and the Qadir quarreled over a strip of borderland called the Sword Marches, which the Fallaji had held three generations before. The Warlord ordered his ornithopters to bomb the dragon engine. The Qadir drew his saber and killed the Warlord on the spot.",
                        "Urza's automaton seized the Qadir by the throat. Ashnod weakened it with her staff. Urza poured the Mightstone's strength into the machine at the same moment Mishra turned the Weakstone on it, and the two forces tore it apart in an explosion. The Fallaji fled across the Kher Ridges with ornithopters harrying them. Kayla became queen of Kroog, and refused to be called Warlady.",
                        "Two years later Mishra came to Kroog itself for a second round of talks. During them he tried to persuade Kayla to steal the Mightstone for him, and Urza found the two of them together in her chambers with his stone. What passed between Mishra and Kayla was never settled, and the doubt would hang over her son for the rest of his life. Urza took back the Mightstone, declared war, and destroyed Mishra's dragon engine, and Mishra fled. Urza spent the next months hunting his brother across the desert. That was exactly what Mishra wanted.",
                        "Four months after the talks, with Urza away and Yotia's capital thinly defended, Mishra struck. He came at the head of the Fallaji with new dragon engines, and Kroog fell in a single morning. Urza's avengers, his finest war machines, were swept aside. In the burning palace Tawnos freed Ashnod, held prisoner since the talks, on her promise to protect the pregnant queen. When the Qadir himself found the three of them and ordered them killed, Ashnod struck down his guards and drained the life out of the Qadir with her staff. Then she put Kayla and Tawnos in a boat headed downriver and went back to Mishra.",
                        "With the Qadir dead, Mishra became Qadir in his place. The people of Yotia had another name for him, the Butcher of Kroog. The card {The Fall of Kroog} calls the attack retaliation for the Warlord's ambush at Korlinda, and in a way it was. It was also the true beginning of a war that would run for more than thirty years.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Those who believed the city of Kroog would never fall to Mishra's forces severely underestimated the might of his war machines.",
                        "Dragon Engine",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "phyrexian-spies", "Phyrexian Spies",
                    listOf(
                        "Mishra's new dragon engines had come from a place he did not understand. Years after his first visit he had gone back to Koilos with Ashnod. The caves were dark and abandoned, but the two of them used a powerstone taken from a su-chi to wake the portal again and stepped through it into [Phyrexia](yawgmoths-spheres). There Mishra bent three of its native engines, the kind later printed as {Phyrexian Dragon Engine}, to his will and brought them home. Something on the far side noticed. A Phyrexian demon caught them at it, and they fled with the portal closing behind them.",
                        "The demon needed a way through. It began to send dreams to a monastery of machine-worshipping monks, showing them visions of a world of perfect metal. They walked across the desert to Koilos, losing several on the way, and twelve of them reached the caves and opened the portal. The demon stepped through and called itself Gix. The Brothers' War novel has the demon take that name from the monks' own worship, but the later novel The Thran made Gix an old servant of [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) from the last days of the Thran, and that is the version now told.",
                        "Some years into the war Gix came to Mishra's fortress to kill him and take the Weakstone. The stone protected itself and its master, threw the demon back and burned his hand. So Gix changed plans. He would not kill Mishra. He would own him. His followers, the Brotherhood of Gix, were welcomed openly in Mishra's realm and soon made up most of his artificers. They became his council. They drove a wedge between Mishra and Ashnod until she was banished from his court. In Urza's lands they worked from the shadows. The cards remember them as {Priest of Gix} and as the oil-veined {Gixian Infiltrator}.",
                        "The war spread across Terisiare. A union of scholars and mages at Terisia City, the Third Path, tried to stand apart from both brothers, and welcomed the Brotherhood of Gix as one of its founding schools. After months of siege by Mishra's armies the Gixians opened the city's gates. The mage Hurkyl died in the fall, her last spell sending three dragon engines away, a spell the world still calls {Hurkyl's Recall}. The Ivory Towers were razed. Outside the Fallaji capital of Tomakul an Argivian army fought a trench war for months and was thrown back within a mile of the walls, and forests went into the furnaces of both brothers to feed machines that were wrecked as fast as they were built.",
                        "Mishra was sick by then, coughing up slime from a disease of the lungs, and the priests of Gix had an answer for weak flesh. They replaced limbs and organs with mechanical versions, and Mishra listened to them in private, sending even Hajar out of the room. Nobody knows exactly when it was done, but before the end Mishra had been given to Gix and compleated. Gix's last move was to draw both armies to an island so rich in timber that neither brother could leave it alone. Its name was [Argoth](argoth).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("After five thousand years, the nightmare returned.", "Portal to Phyrexia"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Antiquities"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Brothers' War"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Brothers' War, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Thran, by J. Robert King"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Brothers' War, Episodes 3 and 4, by Miguel Lopez, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Mishra"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Gix"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Fall of Kroog"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "argoth", "Argoth",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-living-forest", "The Living Forest",
                    listOf(
                        "Argoth was a great forest island off the coast of Terisiare, and the forest was awake. It was bound to the creatures that lived in it and could warn them when strangers came ashore. Elves had lived in the boughs of its trees since the time of the Thran. At the heart of the island stood Citanul, home to an order of druids descended from Thran survivors of that empire's last war, who had turned their backs on technology for good. Centaurs lived there too, and gigantic defenders of wood and earth that the brothers' armies would learn to fear.",
                        "Somewhere on the island was Gaea's Cradle, the place where Argoth was said to have begun to grow. The card {Gaea's Cradle} carries the words of a Citanul elder about it, and they read like a prophecy.",
                        "The island had suffered before. Thousands of years earlier, during his exile from the Thran, [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) had turned a mold native to Argoth into a plague that ate away the elves. He kidnapped their priestess and her healers and demanded a ransom for them and for the cure. When the elves paid, he gave them sweetened water and twelve dead healers. The priestess survived, and later stood among the delegates who demanded his surrender at [Halcyon](halcyon).",
                        "Argoth was ruled by Titania, the Maro-Sorcerer of the forest, a spirit some of its people worshipped as a goddess. She held court at Citanul and kept the island's laws, chief among them that no outsider should harm her trees. The cards have remembered her many times, among them as {Titania, Protector of Argoth}. For centuries nobody on the mainland even knew where her island lay. Then a storm blew one young pilot off course.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Here sprouted the first seedling of Argoth. Here the last tree will fall.", "Gamelen, Citanul elder, on Gaea's Cradle"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "harbins-landing", "Harbin's Landing",
                    listOf(
                        "Harbin was Kayla's son, raised as Urza's, and a flier rather than an artificer. In the fifty-fifth year of the reckoning, at twenty-six, he was flying his ornithopter off the coast of Korlis when a thunderstorm drove him out to sea. He flew for three days without rest, and when he finally came down it was hard, on a beach nobody in his father's kingdoms had ever seen. The card {Harbin, Vanguard Aviator} shows the young pilot eager to fight for his birthright.",
                        "An Argothian named Gwenna found him there. Titania's law said she must send word to Citanul and watch the stranger until an elder arrived, and she did. Harbin meant the island no harm. He only needed a new spur for his damaged machine, so he marked a sapling to cut and went to fetch a rope to haul it. Gwenna knew that if he felled a living tree the island's defenders would kill him. So she found a dead sapling of the same size and kind and dragged it into the clearing in front of the one he had marked.",
                        "When Harbin came back he found a tree already down, used it for his spur, and spared the one he had chosen. He mended his ornithopter and took off the next morning, before the elder reached the beach. Gwenna reported that he had killed no trees, and the elder agreed she had been right to let him go. She was left with the feeling that she had done something she should not have, and the cards would later name her {Gwenna, Eyes of Gaea}.",
                        "Harbin flew home with a story about an island full of untouched timber, in the middle of a war that was eating every forest on the continent.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-harvest", "The Harvest",
                    listOf(
                        "Two years later Harbin came back with the armies of the Combined Kingdoms, the alliance of his father [Urza](who/urza), and they began stripping Argoth of everything it had. Gix made sure Mishra learned of the island too, and the Fallaji followed. The island's defenders fought back against both, and for six years Argoth was a battlefield.",
                        "Titania summoned Harbin to her court at Citanul and ordered him to leave. Harbin tried to make an ally of her instead, arguing that the forest and his father had a common enemy in Mishra. She refused. Urza's armies despoiled her island just as Mishra's did, and she would help nobody who defiled it. She had promised Harbin safe conduct and she kept the promise, though her druids broke all five of his ornithopters and he walked back to his people.",
                        "The fighting ground on. Machines from both sides felled the forest and fed it into their engines, and defenders like the {Citanul Stalwart} fought them tree by tree. The elves, the druids and the creatures of Argoth could not stop them. Before long the two war machines had nearly stripped the island bare.",
                        "Late in those years Ashnod reached Argoth, carrying an ancient bowl she had taken from a scholar of the Third Path. Mishra was unimpressed by it. The priests of Gix wanted it badly, and she gave them a fake. What the real one was, and what it would do, is the story of the [Sylex Blast](sylex-blast). First there was one last battle.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The endless grind of the Brothers' War had fouled Terisiare's land and blackened its skies. Argoth's defenders were determined to protect their isle from the same fate.",
                        "Citanul Stalwart",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "gaeas-champion", "Gaea's Champion",
                    listOf(
                        "The last battle at Argoth was the largest either brother had ever fought, every advanced war machine they owned thrown at the other side. Then Gix took a hand. Using the corruption Phyrexia could work in metal, he drove the artifact creatures of both armies mad, and the battle dissolved into havoc, machines turning on their own side and on anything that moved.",
                        "Titania answered. She drew the last of Argoth's life into herself and became an avatar of Gaea, the spirit of Dominaria's natural world, the figure printed as {Titania, Gaea Incarnate}, and went to war against the machines. Her great duel was with the {Colossus of Sardia}, a towering golem Urza had built from the stone and metal of the Sardian Mountains. Most accounts say Titania and the Colossus destroyed each other, though some have her fall in the blast that ended the battle soon after.",
                        "Urza and Mishra met face to face at last, at the heads of their armies, in the wreck of the forest they had spent six years destroying. Neither the island nor the war would survive what happened next, the catastrophe remembered as [the Sylex Blast](sylex-blast).",
                        "Afterward a few basalt spires were all that remained of Argoth, lashed by the waves and later called the Shattered Isles. A handful of elves eked out a living there and centuries later migrated to Fyndhorn. Citanul druids who had been on the mainland survived the end of their island and went mad with grief, finding peace only in battle. And when Urza came to the forest of Yavimaya, many centuries later, its guardian Multani trapped him inside a tree and made him feel all the pain Titania had felt, a reckoning that belongs to the years of [the Legacy](the-legacy).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Buried under a thin layer of dirt, it was known for centuries as Mount Sardia.", "Colossus of Sardia"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Antiquities"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Brothers' War"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Brothers' War, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Argoth"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Titania"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Invasion of Argoth"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Battle at Argoth"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "sylex-blast", "Sylex Blast",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "golgoth", "Golgoth",
                    listOf(
                        "In the thirty-sixth year of the reckoning, Yumok fishermen pulled something up in their nets and sent it to Feldon, a scholar of languages at Terisia City. It was about a foot across and made of an unknown, copper-colored metal, something between a bowl on a thick pedestal and a squat, wide goblet. Its inside was carved with writing in several languages, Thran, Fallaji and Sumifan among them, which made it a key for translating each into the others.",
                        "The glyphs called the thing a sylex and named its origin as Golgoth. Feldon could not tell whether Golgoth was a place or a maker. His translation of the Fallaji text spoke of wiping the land clean, of bringing the ending and toppling empires to make a fresh start, and of filling the bowl with memories of the land. When Feldon and his colleague Loran first touched it, both felt a darkness come over them, as if the sun had slipped behind an eclipse, and heard a faint scream like a distant child's.",
                        "Nobody really knows where the {Golgothian Sylex} came from. Later tellings say Feldon found it, or Ashnod carved it from a dead man's skull, or Gix dragged it up from the deepest well of oil in Phyrexia, or dwarves hammered it out of the melted heart of a mountain, and the 2022 retelling simply lists the myths and admits there is no way to tell which is true.",
                        "When Mishra's armies broke into Terisia City, Feldon pressed the sylex into Loran's hands and urged her to run. She got out, the escape remembered on {Loran's Escape}. But in the Colekgan Mountains an avalanche smashed her and her companions, and Ashnod, who had set it off, took both Loran and the sylex. Ashnod kept the bowl for years. She never told Mishra she had it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As Terisia City burned, Feldon pressed the sylex into Loran's hands and urged her to run. Hope ran with her.",
                        "Loran's Escape",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ashnods-choice", "Ashnod's Choice",
                    listOf(
                        "Ashnod finally brought the sylex to [Argoth](argoth), hoping it would win back Mishra's favor. He barely looked at it. It needed magic to work, and Mishra wanted machines. The priests of Gix wanted it very much. Ashnod gave them a counterfeit she had made herself, the bowl later known as {Ashnod's Cylix}, and walked out with the real one. Legend would claim she carved the fake from the skull of the Qadir she had killed at Kroog, but it was ordinary coppersmithing.",
                        "In the last battle, with the machines gone mad and the forest dying, Ashnod found Tawnos, Urza's apprentice and her old friend across the lines. Gix found them both. Ashnod handed Tawnos the sylex and stood between him and the demon so he could run. Gix wounded her, but she lived. Tawnos carried the bowl to [Urza](who/urza) with her message, that it should be filled with his memories of the land.",
                        "Urza had just met his brother face to face. Mishra mocked him for growing old, and in that moment Urza saw what he should have seen years before. His brother had not aged a day in years. They fought, and when Mishra was wounded there was machinery under his skin. The Phyrexians had made him one of their own. As Urza watched, Mishra abandoned the last of his humanity and fused his body with a dragon engine, the horror printed as {Mishra, Lost to Phyrexia}.",
                        "Gix's hand had been on the whole war, on both sides of it, from the start. Urza picked up the sylex.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "memories-of-the-land", "Memories of the Land",
                    listOf(
                        "In the novel Urza poured his memories of the land into the bowl, as the inscription asked, while the 2022 retelling has him cut his forehead, fill the sylex with his blood and his grief, and speak the words carved inside it. Either way, it woke.",
                        "The Sylex Blast vaporized all but a fraction of Argoth. It killed both armies, and Mishra with them. It shattered the plate of the world beneath the island and left a trench in the seabed running almost to Sarpadia. Its shock wave crossed Terisiare as far as the Ronom Glacier, where Feldon and Loran, married by then, were living at the foot of the ice. And it threw so much into the sky that the climate of the world began to change. The card {Urza's Ruinous Blast} remembers it in a single line.",
                        "Harbin was at sea, headed for the mainland. A giant wave lifted his ship and smashed it down, and he looked south to see Argoth as a red column of fire. He never reached Penregon, and his mother believed him dead, though years later there were stories in the west of a flying man who had died in the cataclysm and been reborn.",
                        "Tawnos lived because he had climbed into the stasis coffin he had built to imprison Mishra once the war was won, the device printed as {Tawnos's Coffin}. He stayed frozen inside it for years while the world outside began to freeze too.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Centuries ago, one man's vengeance plunged the world into ice and darkness.", "Urza's Ruinous Blast"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "urzas-spark", "Urza's Spark",
                    listOf(
                        "Urza was at the center of it, and his body was destroyed. Then it reknit itself. The two halves of the Koilos stone fused into him and became his eyes, and the spark that had slept in him all his life ignited. Urza had become a planeswalker, one of the rare beings who can step between worlds, the figure printed as {Urza, Planeswalker}. His first walk between the planes was short, and he came back to Dominaria only briefly.",
                        "The 2022 retelling adds a witness. In the present day [Teferi](who/teferi), once Urza's student, sent his spirit back to that moment with a temporal device, searching for the spell that set off the sylex, because his allies needed to use a copy against New Phyrexia. In a moment outside time Urza told him there was no spell. The trigger was a person, in a certain desperate frame of mind. What that knowledge led to belongs to the story of the [Praetors](praetors).",
                        "Urza freed Tawnos from his coffin, asked him to go home to Kayla and tell her what had happened, and left Dominaria. Behind him the world went dark. Debris dimmed the sun, the winters lengthened, and within fifteen years the glaciers had reached Penregon. Worse, the blast tore at the fabric of the multiverse. A barrier called the Shard formed around Dominaria and eleven other worlds and cut them off from every other plane. It kept Urza out. It also kept Phyrexia out, for nearly three thousand years.",
                        "The damage did not stop there. Centuries later the scars of the blast, among other catastrophes, would open [rifts in time](time-rifts) that only the sacrifice of planeswalkers could close. Urza spent the rest of his long life trying to make the destruction mean something, and his first target was [the plane that had taken his brother](yawgmoths-spheres). On Dominaria, meanwhile, the long cold of the [Ice Age](ice-age) was beginning.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("In the end, the war had no winner.", "Terisiare's Devastation"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Antiquities"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Brothers' War"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Brothers' War, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Brothers' War, Episode 5, As Cruel, As Necessary, by Miguel Lopez and Jeff Grubb, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Golgothian Sylex"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Sylex Blast"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Battle at Argoth"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ice-age", "Ice Age",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-dark", "The Dark",
                    listOf(
                        "The centuries after the blast are called the Dark. Debris hung in the sky, the sun dimmed, the summers shortened, and food and fuel grew scarce across Terisiare. In their fear people turned to zealots and despots. The most powerful of the zealots belonged to the Church of Tal, which grew out of two Yotian sun cults and blamed the catastrophe on magic and artifice alike. Its scripture ordered that no magician be suffered to live, and its followers meant it. Mages and artificers were hunted and executed, and those who survived learned to practice in secret.",
                        "The Talites besieged Penregon in the sixty-ninth year, where Kayla bin-Kroog ruled what was left of Argive with Tawnos and his automatons beside her. The city held, but only because its machines were armed and sent out to fight, and Tawnos, sickened to see his creations turned to war again, burned his notes and left. Ten years later the ice reached Penregon and Kayla led her people west. Around then she finished The Antiquities War, the epic poem that became one of the few records of the brothers to survive.",
                        "Tawnos found Ashnod again. Around the eightieth year the two of them founded a hidden school for mages and artificers on the site of the old Monastery of Gix, which grew into the Conclave of Mages, while other survivors of the Third Path built the City of Shadows. Magic did not die in the Dark. It went underground, and it learned.",
                        "Among those who would come to the Conclave, more than three centuries later, was a descendant of Kayla and her lost son Harbin, a young mage named Jodah.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "jodah", "Jodah",
                    listOf(
                        "Jodah was born in the four hundred and thirteenth year, into a landowning family of the old Argive province of Giva whose line ran back to Kayla, and so to [Urza](who/urza) or to Mishra, depending on who had fathered Harbin. The family's fortunes were failing with the summers, and they abandoned their estate when he was a young man. He could channel white mana, and at fifteen he was apprenticed to a wandering mage named Voska, until the Church of Tal caught them both.",
                        "Jodah escaped and wandered. Hiding from goblins in the ruined city of Pitdown, he submerged himself in an abandoned fountain whose waters, it turned out, slowed his aging to a crawl. Years passed before anyone noticed. He learned that Voska had been executed, found his way to the Conclave of Mages, and was caught up in the schemes of its ruler Mairsil, who wanted to use him to open a portal to the dark lands that Jodah's ancestor had supposedly visited. A Talite assault and the release of Mairsil's imprisoned predecessor, Lord Ith, ended Mairsil's reign and tore the Conclave apart. Jodah led its survivors to the City of Shadows.",
                        "He became its Archmage and married the mage Sima. The fountain's gift did not extend to her, and as the Ice Age closed its grip on the world he lost Sima and everyone else he had known to time. Jodah went on, looking no older than twenty-five, for thousands of years. He gathered names the way other men gather scars, Archmage Eternal among them, and he was there for nearly every important event on Terisiare in the age of ice. The card {Jodah, Archmage Eternal} keeps a scholar's doubt that there could have been only one of him.",
                        "Mairsil, as it turned out, was not finished with him either.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Chronicles across the ages describe Jodah. They likely refer not to one mage, but to a family or an arcane title.",
                        "Arkol, Argivian scholar, on Jodah, Archmage Eternal",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lim-dul", "Lim-Dûl",
                    listOf(
                        "Late in the Ice Age the north of Terisiare was divided between the Kjeldorans and the barbarian Balduvians, each sure the other was the great enemy. Both were wrong. A Kjeldoran soldier named Lim-Dûl had fled a battle that slaughtered the rest of his squad, nearly died in a blizzard, and stumbled into the ruins of the Conclave of Mages. There he found Mairsil's ring. It kept him alive and made him a necromancer of terrible power, the figure printed as {Lim-Dûl the Necromancer}, and he built the keep of Tresserhorn where he had found it.",
                        "Lim-Dûl had masters of his own. The planeswalker Leshrac fed him more power, meaning to use his growing undead army to conquer a mana-rich wandering plane called Shandalar, and twisted his body with horns when he showed signs of thinking for himself. Mairsil, locked inside the ring for ages, was slowly taking control of him, hungry for revenge on the last living person who had played a part in his fall. That was Jodah. When a rival betrayed the Archmage, Lim-Dûl clouded his memory with {Fyndhorn Pollen}, convinced him he was a summoned creature who would be banished into nothing if he disobeyed, and set him to researching Shandalar and, in secret, ways to kill a planeswalker.",
                        "Jodah escaped with the help of his old friend Jaya Ballard, the fire mage later printed as {Jaya Ballard, Task Mage}, and the two of them went to Kjeldor to warn its rulers. Few listened until Lim-Dûl overplayed his hand. He had raised a fallen Kjeldoran hero as a zombie and built a secret order around him, the {Knight of Stromgald} and his brothers, and they staged a coup, toasting it with brandy Lim-Dûl had poisoned so that they would all rise as his dead. Jodah saved King Darien, and the shock united Kjeldor behind him and into an alliance with Balduvia.",
                        "The allies survived the winter, and in spring they met Lim-Dûl's army in the last great battle of the Ice Age. Jodah faced the necromancer himself and learned that he was fighting Mairsil as much as Lim-Dûl. The duel left both of them spent. Then Leshrac arrived, furious that the army he wanted had been wasted on Kjeldor. He cut off Lim-Dûl's hand, severing him from the ring, tore him apart while keeping him alive, and took what was left away to be remade. The ice still held the world. Someone else had a plan for that.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Upon the frozen tundra stand the Kjeldoran Royal Guard, pikes raised, with the king's oath upon their lips.",
                        "Kjeldoran Royal Guard",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "world-spell", "The World Spell",
                    listOf(
                        "Freyalise was a planeswalker who had come out of the frozen wastes as a child, a half-elf who kept that secret even from the elves of Fyndhorn and Llanowar, who worshipped her as a goddess for helping them survive the cold. For centuries she had studied the Shard, the barrier the Sylex Blast had thrown around Dominaria, searching for a way to break it. When she learned that Jodah had been forced to research Shandalar, she studied the wandering plane herself and found her answer. The energy of its passage through the Shard, with enough power behind it, could end the Shard and the Ice Age together. The cards remember her as {Freyalise, Llanowar's Fury}.",
                        "Her instrument was the {Ice Cauldron}, a replica of the {Golgothian Sylex}. After the battle she summoned Jodah to Fyndhorn and took his old mirror as payment of a debt, placing it in the cauldron so that it could show the land its true self. The spell she cast was as powerful as the blast that had begun the ice, and it would have been as devastating if Jodah had not spread its force across Terisiare through the hidden safe havens of the mages, destroying every one of them in the process. An older comic told the World Spell as a gentler working powered by a young druid named Kaysa, but the novel The Eternal Ice replaced that version, and the cauldron and the mirror are the official telling.",
                        "The ice began to retreat, and the age called the Thaw began. With the Shard broken, Dominaria was open to the multiverse again, and Phyrexians began to come through new portals almost at once. In Phyrexia, Gix was let out of his torment to try again, and Urza came home to stop him, a story told with [the spheres of Phyrexia](yawgmoths-spheres). The long road to [the Legacy](the-legacy) had begun.",
                        "Freyalise came back to Dominaria for its last wars. Thousands of years later, as time tore open over her beloved forest of Skyshroud, she poured her own power into the wound and died sealing it, one of the stories of the [Time Rifts](time-rifts). Jodah never trusted his mirror again. Freyalise had altered it, and twenty years after the World Spell it would burn the minds of Lim-Dûl and Mairsil out of a possessed Jaya Ballard and ignite her spark.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "The Dark"),
                StorySource("Set", "Ice Age"),
                StorySource("Set", "Alliances"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Gathering Dark, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Eternal Ice, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Shattered Alliance, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Brothers' War, Episodes 1 and 2, by Miguel Lopez, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Ice Age (event)"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Lim-Dûl"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Jodah"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: World Spell"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
